14062	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s2","entry":{"id":"review-9abc72e280e2b535e57ad7622f4ac583","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-23T10:48:41.974Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each of DEF S002 ac1–ac6 is designed: ac1/ac2 by the executor output check at step 4, ac3 by structured tools parsing directly, ac4 by convertText behind a serial queue, ac5 by callTool typed from typebox schemas, ac6 by the keyValues cross-check.","evidence":"DEF-b7a68147dcf72034.json:276-327 lists ac1–ac6 (checked before any caller or rule sees it; mismatch fails and is not passed on; structured output read with no model; cheap model converts text one call after another; typed without a cast; key values cross-checked). Each maps to a contract in LLD §2 and a row in the §6 acceptance mapping. [files: .insrc/artifacts/DEF-b7a68147dcf72034.json]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Narrowing FR001/FR004 (only tools that code calls need a schema, enforced at the callTool type) is backed by a recorded decision and is not an unapproved change of scope.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md:94-98 'Decision: output schemas are required only for tools that code calls (2026-10-23)' gives the replacement FR001/FR004 wording. The DEF text itself still says 'A tool without a declared result shape cannot be registered' (DEF json:331-332), so the DEF amendment is still pending, as LLD §10 says. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, .insrc/artifacts/DEF-b7a68147dcf72034.json]","action":"Have the user accept the decision record as the DEF amendment, or amend S002 FR001/FR004 before approval.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"executeTool does no input or output validation today, wraps tool.execute in a try/catch, and its only production caller is read-only-tool-loop.ts:78, which reads only output and success.","evidence":"executor.ts:23-59: getTool, then an auto-approve log, then try { return await tool.execute } catch → {output:'[Tool failed] ..', success:false, error}. No validation, even though the comment at types.ts:103-107 says 'Schema validation is enforced by the executor'. A grep for executeTool( finds only read-only-tool-loop.ts:78. The loop reads result.output and result.success (lines 134-136). grounding.ts:371 and changed-files.ts:111 call gitDiffTool.execute directly and bypass the executor. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts, src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"withStructuredRetry rethrows AbortError and InputTooLargeError at once and retries any other thrown error. validateAgainstSchema does not mutate (removeAdditional off) and caches validators per schema object.","evidence":"structured-output.ts:215 `if (err.name === 'AbortError') throw err`; :219 the same for 'InputTooLargeError'; :224-229 any other error is turned into a retry. :59-64 ajv uses removeAdditional:false and useDefaults:false; :79-83 says validators are cached per schema object. Note: on a compile failure validateAgainstSchema RETURNS {ok:false, errors:['ajv.compile failed: …']} (:89-93) and does not throw. The LLD's 'throws or reports a compile error' covers both cases. [files: src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts]","action":"In the executor, detect the 'ajv.compile failed:' prefix to tell an invalid schema from a data mismatch.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The tracker maps a gh timeout to 'unreachable' with the message 'timed out after N s', and PortFailureKind has four kinds.","evidence":"github.ts:75-76 `return new GhCommandError('unreachable', `gh timed out after ${GH_TIMEOUT_MS / 1000} s`)`; port.ts:23 `export type PortFailureKind = 'missing-tool' | 'missing-signin' | 'unreachable' | 'rejected'`. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, src/workflow/tracker/port.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"typebox (TSchema/Static) and ajv are runtime dependencies that can be reused. No conversion library and no tools.convert role exist yet.","evidence":"package.json:36 lists '@sinclair/typebox' and :41 lists 'ajv' under dependencies, not devDependencies. The role taxonomy role list (role-taxonomy.ts:59-100) has no tools.convert* id, and nothing in src/daemon/tools has a convertText. [files: package.json, src/config/role-taxonomy.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"RoleRouter.resolveProviderForRole is synchronous and can back ConversionProviders.resolveProvider(role): LLMProvider. Peripheral roles are not clamped by coreFloor.","evidence":"role-router.ts:79 `resolveProviderForRole(role: RoleId, cfg: AnalyzeConfig, repoPath?: string): ResolvedProvider` is synchronous. It returns {provider, resolution} (:62-65), so the helper must unwrap .provider. RoleId is `string` (role-taxonomy.ts:31), so the new ids type-check. core-floor-guard.ts:117 passes peripheral roles through unclamped. [files: src/analyze/context/role-router.ts, src/config/core-floor-guard.ts]","action":"State in the helper that it returns ResolvedProvider.provider and logs resolution, so the 'which role served it' log line has its source.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Exactly four production places build a ToolDeps: driver.ts:1132, ollama.ts:686, grounding.ts:370 and changed-files.ts:110. All of them keep compiling because the conversion field is optional.","evidence":"A grep outside __tests__ finds ollama.ts:686, grounding.ts:370 and changed-files.ts:110. The driver.ts literal is at lines ~1125-1131 (sessionId…requestId:0), close to the cited line. Every other hit is a test cast `as unknown as ToolDeps`. The new field is `conversion?: … | undefined`, so these literals still type-check under exactOptionalPropertyTypes. [files: src/analyze/context/driver.ts, src/agent/providers/ollama.ts, src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts, src/workflow/runners/build/changed-files.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"TypedTool = Tool & {inputSchema: TSchema; outputSchema: TSchema} is compatible with Tool.inputSchema: Record<string, unknown>, and callTool can pass a typed input to executeTool(ToolInput).","evidence":"types.ts:108 `readonly inputSchema: Record<string, unknown>`. A typebox TSchema is an object type, so the intersection holds. types.ts:31 `ToolInput { [key: string]: unknown }` takes Static of an object schema, though callTool may need an internal widening cast, which is allowed. [files: src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every change is an optional additive field (Tool.outputSchema, ToolResult.failure, ToolDeps.conversion). Existing results and the tracker port's stored results are unchanged, and the agent loop sees the same output/success/error.","evidence":"ToolResult today is {output, format, success, error?, data?} (types.ts:33-48). The LLD only adds `failure?`. The loop reads only output and success (read-only-tool-loop.ts:134-136). PortFailureKind and PortResult (port.ts:23-27) are left untouched, and nothing persists a ToolResult. [files: src/daemon/tools/types.ts, src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts, src/workflow/tracker/port.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD does not redesign s1's hook stage or s3's approval step. It declares ToolDenialReason locally, and the sc5 additions go through an HLD amendment.","evidence":"LLD §2.2 preconditions leave steps 1-3 (resolve, approval, run) to the executor and s3, and hooks at step 5 to s1. AMD-b7a68147dcf72034-1/-2/-3.json all target contractId 'sc5', storyId 's2', status 'draft'. Note there are three draft sc5 amendments, which look like duplicates from repeated runs. [files: .insrc/artifacts/AMD-b7a68147dcf72034-1.json, .insrc/artifacts/AMD-b7a68147dcf72034-2.json, .insrc/artifacts/AMD-b7a68147dcf72034-3.json]","action":"Before approval, keep one sc5 amendment and withdraw the other two drafts.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.2","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The shared contracts sc2 and sc3, as consumed by s4–s7, match what the LLD implements.","evidence":"In HLD-b7a68147dcf72034.json:112 the sc2 sketch is `outputSchema?: JsonSchema; readonly outputType?: O … callTool<T extends OutputSchemaField<any> & { id: string }>(tool: T, input: ToolInput<T>, deps)`. At :129 the sc3 sketch is `convertText<O>(req: ConversionRequest<O>)`, which takes no deps and uses schema: JsonSchema. The LLD changes both: a typebox TSchema, the TypedTool constraint, Static input, and a deps parameter on convertText. No amendment is filed: every AMD for this epic targets sc5 only. LLD §10 admits this. Consumers s4–s7 reading the HLD would build against the old signatures. [files: .insrc/artifacts/HLD-b7a68147dcf72034.json]","action":"File sharedContract amendments for sc2 and sc3 (or a back-flow) carrying the typebox/TypedTool/deps refinements before s4–s7 are designed.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"A conversion makes at most two attempts per tier, each bounded by a per-attempt deadline, with retries counted by withStructuredRetry.","evidence":"LLMProvider.completeStructured retries internally: shared/types.ts:290-291 `maxAttempts … Default 3`, and :322-325 `internalRetry … Absent or true: today's retries`. It also has its own `timeoutMs` (:320-321) and `signal` (:293). The LLD wraps the provider call in withStructuredRetry with two attempts but never says it passes `maxAttempts: 1` / `internalRetry: false`. With the defaults, each of the LLD's 'attempts' becomes up to three provider attempts plus transient retries. That breaks the 'at most two attempts per tier' postcondition, and the per-attempt deadline would cover several hidden retries. [files: src/shared/types.ts, src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts]","action":"State that convertText calls completeStructured with internalRetry:false (and maxAttempts:1), passes a signal combining the deadline and deps.signal, and uses withStructuredRetry as the only retry loop. Add a unit test asserting the fake provider sees exactly 2+2 calls.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The unknown-tool and thrown-error paths already exist in executeTool and can carry failure kind 'rejected' without changing their text.","evidence":"executor.ts:29-37 returns {output:'Unknown tool: …', success:false, error:\"No tool registered for '…'\"}, and :49-57 returns the caught-throw result. Adding `failure` to both is purely additive. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The ac5 type-level assertions in src/daemon/tools/call-tool.type-test.ts are compiled by `npm run build`, because tsx does not type-check and __tests__ is excluded.","evidence":"tsconfig.json:23-30 includes 'src/**/*.ts' and excludes '**/__tests__/**' and '**/*.test.ts'. 'call-tool.type-test.ts' ends in '-test.ts', not '.test.ts', so tsc compiles it (package.json:15 `build: tsc && …`). The file is also emitted to out/, which is harmless since it is never imported. [files: tsconfig.json, package.json]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.2","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every acceptance criterion ac1–ac6 maps to at least one named test.","evidence":"The LLD §6 acceptance mapping has rows ac1–ac6, each naming test cases in output-schema.test.ts, call-tool.integration.test.ts, convert-text.test.ts, call-tool.type-test.ts or convert-text.live.test.ts. Some mapped titles, such as 'the check runs before the result is returned to the caller', are not in the unit subject list word for word. The build's test mapping must use the exact titles.","action":"Make the acceptance-mapping titles match the unit subject titles exactly, so the validate step's test mapping resolves.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":2,"low":14,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-23T10:48:41.974Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
